WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.391
Book Three, Proposition Thirty-Two.

2
00:00:02.391 --> 00:00:16.472
If a straight line touch a circle, and from the point of contact there be drawn across, in the circle, a straight line cutting the circle, the angles which it makes with the tangent will be equal to the angles in the alternate segments of the circle.

3
00:00:16.472 --> 00:00:23.850
Let the straight line E F touch the circle A B C D at the point B, and from B let the chord B D be drawn across cutting the circle.

4
00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.860
From B, draw B A at right angles to E F by One Eleven.

5
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:34.640
Take a point C at random on the arc B D, and join A D, D C, and C B.

6
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:46.157
Because E F touches the circle at B and B A is drawn from the point of contact at right angles to the tangent, the center of the circle lies on B A by Three Nineteen.

7
00:00:46.157 --> 00:00:54.046
Therefore B A is a diameter of the circle, and by Three Thirty-One the angle A D B in the semicircle is a right angle.

8
00:00:54.046 --> 00:01:03.672
By One Thirty-Two, the three angles of triangle A B D equal two right angles, so the remaining two angles B A D and A B D equal one right angle.

9
00:01:03.672 --> 00:01:08.886
But the angle A B F is also right, so A B F equals the sum of B A D and A B D.

10
00:01:08.886 --> 00:01:17.644
Subtract the common angle A B D from each; the remaining angle D B F equals the angle B A D in the alternate segment of the circle.

11
00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:26.602
Again, because A B C D is a quadrilateral in a circle, its opposite angles B A D and B C D equal two right angles by Three Twenty-Two.

12
00:01:26.602 --> 00:01:32.952
The adjacent angles D B F and D B E on the tangent also equal two right angles by One Thirteen.

13
00:01:32.952 --> 00:01:42.980
Subtracting the equal angles D B F and B A D leaves the angle D B E equal to the angle D C B in the alternate segment D C B, as was required to prove.

14
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:53.554
Proposition Three Thirty-Two is the Alternate Segment Theorem: the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the inscribed angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc.

15
00:01:53.554 --> 00:02:05.049
As Heath observes, the converse holds with equal elegance, and Pappus used this theorem to prove that any line through the point of contact of two tangent circles cuts off similar segments in each.

16
00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:21.680
